text:
kevin (CS): Hey team — customer (AcmeAnalytics) opened CS-4872. They're seeing SSE streams from our hosted API drop ~60s into long responses. Symptoms: partial output, client reconnects then resumes but sometimes duplicates tokens. They see intermittent 502/504 coming from their ALB in front of our LB. Repro steps they shared below. Really high priority for them, billing impacted because they do many long streams.

rebecca (Support): Thanks, Kevin. Can you paste the full request id or any server-side request_id we can match? Also which model and route? Any CDN (Cloudflare) in front of ALB?

kevin (CS): Request-ID: rwd-req-05d2e9a1. Model: redwood-open-7b; route: /v1/stream. Customer topology: Cloudflare (fronting) -> AWS ALB -> our internal NLB -> service -> runtime. They confirmed Cloudflare on their DNS + ALB default idle timeout (60s).

kevin (CS): Customer's curl repro:
```
# client side repro they ran from their box
curl -N -H 'Accept: text/event-stream' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer xxxxx' \
  'https://api.redwood.example/v1/stream?model=redwood-open-7b'
```
They get streaming chunks, then around 55-65s connection resets. Attached raw tcpdump shows FIN from ALB.

ashok (eng-sre): 2 quick things to check: ALB idle timeout (default 60s) will close if no bytes cross the wire for that interval. Cloudflare can also buffer responses (it will often buffer until the response is complete unless using WebSockets). If the runtime sends long pauses between data chunks (e.g., batching token emission), middle proxies may tear.

tara (eng-runtime): Adding runtime context: our default token flush cadence for large generations can be bursty — we batch a few tokens then flush. If there's >60s pause between flushes, ALB will drop. We usually send a heartbeat (comment line) for SSE to keep connections alive but not all models trigger it in older runtimes.

rebecca (Support): Customer also says after the reconnect they sometimes see duplicate prefix tokens. That matches scenario where client retries without idempotent resume. Need to recommend best immediate mitigations for them.

ashok (eng-sre): Suggested immediate mitigations for customer (customer-facing):
- Set ALB idle timeout to >= 180s (AWS Console -> Listeners -> Idle timeout). ALB default is 60s.
- If using Cloudflare, try bypassing Cloudflare (point an /api host to an ALB CNAME directly) to check whether CF buffering is the cause. Cloudflare often buffers HTTP responses on HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 and may not stream tokens in real-time on all plans.
- If they run nginx in front of ALB, ensure:
  proxy_buffering off;
  proxy_http_version 1.1;
  proxy_set_header Connection ""; # don't force close
  chunked_transfer_encoding on;
- If Envoy is in path, set stream_idle_timeout to a value > expected idle and ensure max upstream response size not causing buffering.

tara (eng-runtime): From our side we can enable heartbeat pings for that customer's tenant on the runtime: sending a SSE comment line every 20s like `: heartbeat\n\n` or a tiny event `event: ping\ndata: {}`\n\n will keep bytes flowing so ALB/CF won't think the connection is idle. Also we can reduce batching to flush smaller token windows. I'll spin a patch to enable tenant-level heartbeat toggles.

kevin (CS): Customer asked for config snippets to forward to their infra team. Can we produce copy-paste-able examples?

ashok (eng-sre): Sure — nginx example: 
```nginx
location /v1/stream/ {
  proxy_pass http://upstream_runtime;
  proxy_buffering off;
  proxy_cache off;
  proxy_http_version 1.1;
  proxy_set_header Connection "";
  chunked_transfer_encoding on;
  proxy_read_timeout 300s; 
}
```
ALB: set Idle timeout to 180s (console or CLI `aws elbv2 modify-load-balancer-attributes --load-balancer-arn ... --attributes Key=idle_timeout.timeout_seconds,Value=180`).

Cloudflare note (rebecca): If they must keep CF in front, they should switch to a zone config that bypasses cache for the /v1/stream path and enable "Origin Pull" (or use a subdomain that bypasses CF). Free plan may still buffer. Recommend a quick test: `curl -iH 'Accept: text/event-stream' https://bypass-cname.example/v1/stream` to compare latencies/behavior.

kevin (CS): I ran the customer's tcpdump and saw FIN from ALB exactly 61s after last byte — matches ALB idle. Customer will try increasing ALB timeout and disabling Cloudflare for the test domain.

tara (eng-runtime): I'll deploy a tenant toggle for heartbeat (20s) to the canary runtime lane for rwd-req-05d2e9a1 so we can see if that alone prevents the ALB close. Patch linked: https://git.internal/redwood/runtime/pull/4123 (canary only).

deploy-bot: Deploy started for runtime/canary.

deploy-bot: Deploy succeeded — runtime/canary at sha f1a2b3c (heartbeat toggle available).

rebecca (Support): Customer tested after we enabled heartbeat on canary + they bumped ALB to 180s and bypassed CF — result: stable streaming, no disconnects for 10 minutes. They reported duplicate token rate dropped to 0 (client-side dedupe no longer needed).

kevin (CS): Great. Action items: 
- Customer to keep ALB timeout >= 120s in prod.
- Customer to either bypass Cloudflare for streaming subdomain or upgrade to a plan that explicitly supports streaming / edge streaming.
- eng-runtime to add tenant-level heartbeat flag and default to on for hosted customers with long-stream usage; move from canary -> prod after monitoring.
- eng-sre to add docs KB "SSE streaming and proxy timeouts" with examples for ALB/NLB/Envoy/nginx/Cloudflare.

tara (eng-runtime): Will open PR to make heartbeat default for hosted-only tenants and include metrics for heartbeat bytes emitted. PR: https://git.internal/redwood/runtime/pull/4126

ashok (eng-sre): I'll draft the KB and add sample configs + reproduce steps (including `curl -N` and a tiny node server to reproduce). Repro server: 
```js
// very small express SSE server to reproduce idle-timeout
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.get('/sse', (req, res) => {
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');
  res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache');
  res.flushHeaders();
  res.write(': hello\n\n'); // initial comment to handshake
  let i = 0;
  const interval = setInterval(() => {
    res.write(`data: token-${i}\\n\\n`);
    i++;
    if (i > 100) { clearInterval(interval); res.end(); }
  }, 30000); // 30s between token bursts -> will trigger ALB 60s default if misconfigured
});
app.listen(3000);
```
Repro instructions: run server, curl -N against it via customer's proxy chain.

kevin (CS): Customer is happy and will keep ticket open for 48h to confirm. We'll mark resolved after that. Thanks everyone. :thumbsup:

rebecca (Support): Saved notes to CS-4872 and KB draft in confluence. Closing loop with customer in 24h.

tara (eng-runtime): Follow-up: I'll monitor canary logs for any increase in connection churn. If everything looks good, I'll promote heartbeat toggle to prod in 48h.

ashok (eng-sre): KB action: I'll include a checklist: check ALB idle, check Cloudflare buffering, disable nginx proxy_buffering, set Envoy stream_idle_timeout, and add the heartbeat pattern.

kevin (CS): Leaving the thread open for now.
